# URLs lychee must not treat as broken. One regex per line; blank lines and
# lines starting with # are comments.
#
# Everything below is a URL a CORRECT document may legitimately contain and that
# an unauthenticated fetch cannot succeed against. Without these entries the
# weekly check reports them as broken, and a link checker that cries wolf is a
# link checker someone deletes.
#
# SCOPE, measured 2026-08-10: lychee extracts links from prose, not from fenced
# code blocks or inline code spans. Today every URL below sits inside one of
# those -- README.md:94-95, scripts/s3-structure.md:217,237, backend/README.md's
# example SAM output, and the localhost lines in four documents -- so none of
# these entries fires yet. They are here for the day one is written as prose or
# as a link, which is exactly when an unexplained red would arrive.

# The CloudFront distribution serving the archive. A key that exists returns 200
# (verified 2026-08-10: /public/2015/03/20150315.json), but the origin access
# grants no s3:ListBucket, so the distribution root and every missing key return
# 403 rather than 404 -- root verified 403 the same day. Paths quoted in the docs
# are illustrations of key shape, not promises that a given object is there.
^https?://d3vq6af2mo7fcy\.cloudfront\.net

# Not a scheme any HTTP client can fetch.
^s3://

# README.md's environment table names this host to show the SHAPE of
# VITE_API_BASE_URL. It deliberately does not exist; DNS resolution fails.
^https?://placeholder-api-gateway\.amazonaws\.com

# backend/README.md quotes example SAM stack output. The account id in it is
# made up and no such API Gateway stage exists.
^https?://[a-z0-9]+\.execute-api\.[a-z0-9-]+\.amazonaws\.com

# Developer instructions. Nothing listens on these ports on a CI runner.
^https?://localhost(:[0-9]+)?
^https?://127\.0\.0\.1(:[0-9]+)?
